WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.427
A small shop keeps its stockroom at the back.

2
00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:11.125
Opening it takes a while: find the key, switch on the lights, pull the covers off the shelves.

3
00:00:11.125 --> 00:00:16.459
Most days nobody needs it, so the shopkeeper opens it only when the first customer asks.

4
00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:21.631
Every card number starts with six digits that say which bank issued it.

5
00:00:21.631 --> 00:00:26.032
The shop's program keeps a table from those digits to the bank's name.

6
00:00:26.032 --> 00:00:32.634
Loading it reads a big file, about 50 milliseconds, so the program builds it the first time someone asks.

7
00:00:32.634 --> 00:00:34.772
That's called lazy initialization.

8
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.811
The first version checks, then acts: if the table is null, build it.

9
00:00:39.811 --> 00:00:42.156
Now give the shop several assistants.

10
00:00:42.156 --> 00:00:46.023
In Java, they're threads, paths of work running side by side.

11
00:00:46.023 --> 00:00:48.368
Two customers ask at the same moment.

12
00:00:48.368 --> 00:00:51.981
Two assistants see the door shut, and both go to open it.

13
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.465
Two tables get built.

14
00:00:53.465 --> 00:00:55.609
Here that wastes 50 milliseconds.

15
00:00:55.609 --> 00:01:00.483
If opening meant connecting to another computer, one connection would leak.

16
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.524
The simple fix is one key on a hook by the counter.

17
00:01:04.524 --> 00:01:08.566
Every assistant takes the key before going near the stockroom.

18
00:01:08.566 --> 00:01:13.585
In Java, that's a synchronized method: its lock lets one thread in at a time.

19
00:01:13.585 --> 00:01:14.432
It's correct.

20
00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:19.979
But every assistant takes the key on every visit, even when the door has stood open since nine.

21
00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:23.992
By lunch, the queue for the key is longer than the queue for the till.

22
00:01:23.992 --> 00:01:28.463
Every call takes a lock, for a table that never changes after the first build.

23
00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:33.160
Wouldn't it be nice to glance at the door first, and take the key only if it's shut?

24
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:35.064
So here's the plan.

25
00:01:35.064 --> 00:01:39.477
Glance at the door first: if the table is there, use it, with no lock.

26
00:01:39.477 --> 00:01:40.801
That's the fast path.

27
00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:46.537
If not, take the key, and check again, because someone may have opened it while you waited.

28
00:01:46.537 --> 00:01:48.428
Only then, open the stockroom.

29
00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:53.294
Checking once outside the lock and once inside is called double-checked locking.

30
00:01:53.294 --> 00:01:57.400
The lock and the second check make sure only one table is ever built.

31
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:00.772
Here's a question that feels silly to ask.

32
00:02:00.772 --> 00:02:03.411
We check twice, and we build under a lock.

33
00:02:03.411 --> 00:02:05.044
What could still go wrong?

34
00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:07.085
Look at the fast path.

35
00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:13.017
An assistant who glances, sees the door open, and walks a customer in never touches the key.

36
00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:17.079
So nothing links that glance to the work the opener did inside.

37
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.030
Let's slow it down.

38
00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.943
Thread 1 holds the lock and builds the table.

39
00:02:21.943 --> 00:02:31.784
The JIT and the processor are free to let the reference out first, and the table's rows a moment later, like the envelope that arrived before its pages.

40
00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:33.661
Thread 2 takes the fast path.

41
00:02:33.661 --> 00:02:37.545
It sees a table that isn't null, reads its rows, and gets 0.

42
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:41.163
The door is open, and the lights are off.

43
00:02:41.163 --> 00:02:44.876
Somewhere in the dark, a customer is choosing socks by touch.

44
00:02:44.876 --> 00:02:49.503
In code, the reference isn't null, and the fields behind it are still empty.

45
00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:55.813
A stress tester from the team that builds Java runs exactly this, on an ARM machine.

46
00:02:55.813 --> 00:03:01.326
Two threads ask a broken double check for the same object, about 2.3 billion times.

47
00:03:01.326 --> 00:03:05.976
How often does one get an object that isn't null, but carries no data?

48
00:03:08.967 --> 00:03:10.689
About 1.8 million times.

49
00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:11.765
Rare, and real.

50
00:03:11.765 --> 00:03:18.582
And when the test made the data field final, there were no failures at all, on ARM or on Intel.

51
00:03:18.582 --> 00:03:28.556
That's why the broken version so often seems to work: many objects have only final fields, and Intel and AMD machines rarely show the swap.

52
00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:34.641
So checking twice without volatile can hand out an object that looks finished, and isn't.

53
00:03:34.641 --> 00:03:37.963
The fast path needs an edge, and one word gives it one.

54
00:03:38.567 --> 00:03:43.450
Make the field volatile, and the door gets a sign, like the museum's OPEN sign.

55
00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:49.754
The assistant who opens the stockroom hangs OPEN last, after the lights are on and the covers are off.

56
00:03:49.754 --> 00:03:53.648
Everyone else trusts the sign, never a glance through the door.

57
00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:59.914
The volatile write that stores the table is a release: it sends everything written before it along.

58
00:03:59.914 --> 00:04:05.876
The volatile read on the fast path is an acquire: when it sees the table, it receives all of it.

59
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:11.372
Where the picture breaks: in a real shop, you'd notice a dark room at once.

60
00:04:11.372 --> 00:04:15.381
In the JVM, a half-built object looks just like a finished one.

61
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.226
Let's run the fixed version: eight threads ask at the same moment.

62
00:04:20.226 --> 00:04:22.980
One load, and all eight get the same table.

63
00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.773
Now delete the second check.

64
00:04:24.773 --> 00:04:25.797
How many tables?

65
00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:30.677
Eight loads, eight different tables.

66
00:04:30.677 --> 00:04:34.912
Every assistant who queued for the key opened the stockroom again.

67
00:04:34.912 --> 00:04:36.131
Both checks matter.

68
00:04:36.233 --> 00:04:39.225
Put it back, and this time remove volatile.

69
00:04:39.225 --> 00:04:40.129
What changes?

70
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:43.068
Nothing.

71
00:04:43.068 --> 00:04:45.410
One load, one table, every round.

72
00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:51.301
A test like this is a smoke alarm with no battery: calm, quiet, and no help at all.

73
00:04:51.301 --> 00:04:55.276
On an Intel laptop, the missing edge almost never shows.

74
00:04:55.276 --> 00:04:58.896
Only a stress tester, ideally on ARM, can catch it.

75
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.749
Here's the fixed version, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

76
00:05:03.749 --> 00:05:06.412
Read the volatile field once, into a local.

77
00:05:06.412 --> 00:05:09.199
If it's null, take the lock, and check again.

78
00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.222
So what went wrong with checking twice?

79
00:05:12.222 --> 00:05:15.555
The glance at the door takes no key, so it needs a sign.

80
00:05:15.555 --> 00:05:19.008
volatile is that sign: the edge the fast path was missing.

81
00:05:19.133 --> 00:05:19.991
Quick check.

82
00:05:19.991 --> 00:05:25.640
Your double check has no volatile, and a thousand runs all pass on your laptop.

83
00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:26.641
Is it correct?
